# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repository is edited on Windows and some of its files are written by
# scripts running on Linux, so without a policy the same file arrives with CRLF
# from one and LF from the other and git reports every line as changed. Pinning
# it here means a diff only ever shows a real edit.

# Default: git stores LF, and checks out whatever the platform uses.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts must keep LF. With CRLF, Linux reads the carriage return as part
# of the interpreter path and fails with "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" — a
# confusing error for a script that looks fine.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Batch files are the mirror image: CRLF, so cmd.exe behaves.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Results and source: stored LF, checked out native.
*.py   text
*.md   text
*.csv  text
*.json text
*.txt  text

# Binary — never convert anything in these.
*.png binary
*.pdf binary
*.pt  binary
*.npy binary
